-- title:
--   Rotating a cone axis, and scaling as an equivalence of $\mathbb{R}^d$
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$. For unit vectors $u$ and $w$ with $w$ orthogonal to $u$, and $t \in \mathbb{R}$, the **rotated axis** is
--
--   $$\mathrm{rot}(u,w,t) \;=\; \cos t \cdot u \;+\; \sin t \cdot w,$$
--
--   the rotation of $u$ by the angle $t$ in the plane it spans with $w$; letting $t$ vary sweeps a one-parameter family of axes inside a cone about $u$. Separately, for $h \ne 0$, **scaling** is packaged as the equivalence $x \mapsto h^{-1}x$ of $\mathbb{R}^d$ with itself, with inverse $y \mapsto h\,y$ — the map carrying the rescaled lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ onto $\mathbb{Z}^d$.
--
--   The two are the ingredients of the thin-cone lemma in dimension at least two: a shrunk reference cone omits every lattice point of norm at most $r/\sin\theta$, only finitely many lattice points are that short, and rotating the axis along a one-parameter family meets each of those finitely many directions at most once — so some rotation avoids all of them, and the apex angle can then be shrunk below the least of the resulting angles.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The existence of a direction orthogonal to the axis is the only place dimension at least two is used, and the file records that the corresponding statement is *false* in dimension one at the radius where it is applied: there every double cone is all of $\mathbb{R}\setminus\{0\}$, so a thin cone meets $\pm 1$ while the shrunk cone omits them. The rotation is defined for arbitrary $u$, $w$, $t$ with no unit-norm or orthogonality hypothesis; those are supplied where it is used. The scaling equivalence is noncomputable and requires only $h \ne 0$.
